#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065276} :activity/id #uuid "273c64d6-9225-51a9-8513-982dcfdd1973" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048546 :topic/id #uuid "a8d02ba6-b6bf-5189-bb4e-2eec42cf7133" :topic/math-academy-id 329} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065430 :tutorial/content "There are two ways to add a positive number to a negative number. Let's work out the value of $-5+7$ using both methods.\n\n**Method 1 - Picturing a Number Line**\n\nWhen we add, we move to the right on the number line.\n\nSo, to add $7$ to $-5,$ we start at $-5$ on a number line and move $7$ spaces to the right.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f99b09208d09824dc7c4e6dfaafdc0e20feb7fde00f821a57a70d79196936d.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we do this, we land at $2.$ Therefore, $-5+7 =2.$\n\n**Method 2 - Subtraction in Reverse**\n\nAdding a positive number to a negative number is like *subtraction in reverse*. To turn the addition into a subtraction, we must switch the numbers around.\n\nSo, to work out ${\\color{red}{-5}} + {\\color{blue}{7}},$ we can switch the numbers around to get \n$\\,{\\color{blue}{7}} {\\color{red}{\\,-\\,5}} =2.$\n\nWe're allowed to swap the numbers because addition is **commutative**, which means the order in which we add two numbers does not matter." :tutorial/id #uuid "fea61476-1945-5134-8063-3e50b2865f8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8382 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1c01112c-13ce-5b10-8825-cd205fd87939" :step/math-academy-id 74975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064354 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063965 :question/id #uuid "4d61f8ae-cf47-5aa7-96f8-770a232509ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-13380"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09d49af8-8aed-5434-bbf4-12c90c658fc0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048911}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445414}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding a Positive Number to a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "d317273f-64d9-5382-91e1-beb5ef25c23c" :step/math-academy-id 74976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064355 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063966 :question/id #uuid "a92db588-62a8-5a19-8420-a38d25dd9088" :question/math-academy-id "e-13381"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b20f6b97-6531-5ece-a1d0-4d25d4895dab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445415}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding a Positive Decimal to a Negative Decimal"} :step/id #uuid "fdbb7f5d-0de1-5533-a248-8d52ffb6de29" :step/math-academy-id 74979 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065431 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate $-\\dfrac 1 3 + \\dfrac 5 3.$\n\nSince both fractions have a common denominator of $3,$ we can combine them to form one fraction:\n\n \n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{3} + \\dfrac{5}{3} =\\dfrac{-1+5}{3}\n$$\n\nTo compute the numerator, we can use either method that we've learned.\n\n**Method 1 - Picturing a Number Line**\n\nTo add $5$ to $-1,$ we start at $-1$ on a number line and move $5$ spaces to the right.\n\n![](images/63/632e4bcce5b619f9a5c4dfb2c414a33da0e9567b7b4d8b15adb439da1b3dd88d.png)\n\nWe end up at $4.$ So $-1+5=4,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-1+5}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Subtraction in Reverse**\n\nWe can swap the order of the numbers in the numerator and then compute the resulting subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1+5}{3}=\\frac{5-1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $-\\dfrac1 3 + \\dfrac 5 3 = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\n**Note:** If two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator *first* and *then* subtract!" :tutorial/id #uuid "9522d6a9-a066-57d7-bf8a-e4baf0467548" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8383 :tutorial/title "Adding a Positive Fraction to a Negative Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "51a3f9a7-44e9-5617-afa0-b6145e79910d" :step/math-academy-id 74983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064356 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063967 :question/id #uuid "a287913a-827a-56fa-8bbf-5f716dff277e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13382"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "169a836e-5209-53a1-bda9-0542b044819d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048639}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445411}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding a Positive Fraction to a Negative Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "fb6f62cc-a440-5782-82f3-8987d7d10530" :step/math-academy-id 74984}] :activity/title "Adding a Positive Number to a Negative Number" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064420}]}
